<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Camille Pissarro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a>–<a href="/years/1903">1903</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Jacob Abraham Camille Pissarro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Camille Pissarro was a Danish-French Impressionist and Neo-Impressionist painter, born in 1830 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlotte-amalie">Charlotte Amalie</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-thomas-u-s-virgin-islands">Saint Thomas</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-virgin-islands">Danish West Indies</a>. He learned from Gustave Courbet and Jean-Baptiste-Camille Corot, and in 1873 helped establish a collective society of fifteen artists, becoming the figure who held the group together; he is the only artist who showed at all eight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Impressionist exhibitions, from 1874 to 1886. At 54 he took up the Neo-Impressionist style alongside Georges Seurat and Paul Signac, and he acted as a father figure to Cézanne, Seurat, Gauguin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vincent-van-gogh">Van Gogh</a>. Éragny was his principal home until he died in Paris in 1903.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/camille-pissarro.jpg" alt="Camille Pissarro" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pissarro-portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jacob Abraham Camille Pissarro was born on 10 July 1830 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlotte-amalie">Charlotte Amalie</a> to Frederick Abraham Gabriel Pissarro, a merchant of Portuguese Jewish descent who held French nationality, and Rachel Manzano-Pomié. His father's marriage to his late uncle's widow caused a stir in the small Jewish community, and the four children were sent to an all-black primary school. In 1855, at 25, Camille moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and worked as an assistant to the painter Anton Melbye. He found the teaching at the École des Beaux-Arts and the Académie Suisse stifling and asked Corot for instruction instead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His first painting was accepted by the Paris Salon in 1859. Corot inspired him to paint outdoors, and at the Académie Suisse he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-monet">Claude Monet</a>, Armand Guillaumin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-cezanne">Paul Cézanne</a>, who shared his dissatisfaction with the Salon. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> he moved his family to Norwood near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, since he had only Danish nationality. Of the 1,500 paintings he left in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, only 40 remained, since soldiers had used many as floor mats. In 1871 he married Julie Vellay, with whom he had seven children, six of them painters. The family settled at Éragny-sur-Epte in April 1884.</p><p>The first Impressionist exhibition, in 1874, shocked critics, who found the subjects vulgar and the brushwork sketchy. Pissarro showed five landscapes, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emile-zola">Émile Zola</a> praised them. In 1885 he met Georges Seurat and Paul Signac and for about three years used their method of small dabs of pure colour, showing such works in 1886 at the eighth exhibition. By the end of the 1880s he found the method too restrictive, but kept a stronger attention to colour. He was also drawn to anarchist circles, and in 1889 made an album of thirty drawings, Turpitudes sociales.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1892 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-monet">Monet</a> helped Pissarro and Julie buy the Éragny house, and in 1893 Pissarro converted the barn into a studio. In old age a recurring eye infection kept him from working outdoors except in warm weather, so he painted from hotel windows in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, Le Havre, Dieppe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. He died in Paris on 13 November 1903, aged 73, of sepsis, and was buried in Père Lachaise Cemetery. The art historian Diane Kelder wrote that no member of the Impressionist group did more to mediate the disputes that threatened to break it apart. Cézanne said that Pissarro had been a father for him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Camille Pissarro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the Café Guerbois in Paris their discussions were often led by Édouard Manet, and they were joined by <b>Camille Pissarro</b>, Paul Cézanne and Armand Guillaumin.</blockquote><a href="/movement/impressionism">Impressionism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>In the arts anarchism was close to the futurists and surrealists and to the painter <b>Camille Pissarro</b>, and later to punk bands such as Crass and the Sex Pistols.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/anarchism">Anarchism · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Charlotte Amalie. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.35,-64.95&amp;year=1877" aria-label="Charlotte Amalie on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Camille Pissarro"><image href="/maps/globes/g300n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4599 5981h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4599 5981h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=18.35,-64.95&amp;year=1877">What was here in 1877? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
